WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.325 --> 00:00:02.750
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Deconstructing Chatbots.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.280
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.840
we will learn how to integrate a
Dialogflow agent with BigQuery.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.710
If you have not checked
out our previous episode

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.720
on fulfillment, I
highly encourage

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.400
you to watch that before
you continue here.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.030
Because we are going to use
our same appointment scheduler

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.070
Chatbots that
creates appointments

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.200
in Google Calendar.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.410
Today, let's enhance it to
send appointment information

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.530
to BigQuery to gain some
insights from this appointment

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.220
data.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.460
So let's jump into
our Dialogflow console

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.590
and see how to set
up the fulfillment

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:44.390
to send data to BigQuery.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.170
Open Appointment Scheduler
Agent in the Dialogflow console

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.240
and navigate to Fulfillment.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.800
In order to integrate
with BigQuery,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.590
the first thing we
need to make sure

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:59.530
is to open package.json and
add the BigQuery dependency.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.750
Now, before we make
edits to the index.js,

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.650
let's first set up our BigQuery
data set in GCP console.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:10.940
Under Resources on the left
pane, click on the project ID.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.840
Once selected, you will see
Create Data Set on the right.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.630
Click on Create Data
Set and name it.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:20.210
Once the data set is
created, click on it

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.870
from the left panel.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.310
You will create table
on the right now.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.860
Provide table name
and create the table.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.840
Click on the table
and edit the schema.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.610
Add date, time, and type as the
three fields for appointment

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.230
time and the appointment type.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.180
Take note of your
project ID, data

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.820
set ID, table ID, and
the schema fields.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.870
We will need all those
in our fulfillment.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.780
index.js code is linked
in the description below.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.440
First, create a
BigQuery constant.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.380
We will scroll through the
Google Calendar setup code

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.420
for creating calendar invite.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.890
Please watch the
previous episode

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.979
to learn how to integrate
Google Calendar with Dialogflow

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:11.720
Fulfillment.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.200
In our Make Appointment function
after the calendar event

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.200
is set, we are calling
our BigQuery function,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.290
Add to BigQuery, which will
add the same appointment

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.650
information into the BigQuery
data set we just created.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.790
Let's check out the
function, Add to Dialogflow.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.210
We are passing in the agent
and the appointment type.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.210
Then we are modifying
the date and time

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.760
into a readable format.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.930
Uncomment the code and add
the project ID, data set ID,

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.080
and table ID to make
that BigQuery connection.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.850
We are using our schema fields
for date, time, and appointment

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:54.080
type to create a row
entry into BigQuery.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.260
At this point, we have all
the information we need.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.370
We call our BigQuery
object to add the row in.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.000
We finally catch if
there are any errors.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.720
Once you've made the required
edits to the index.js,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.930
copy and paste it into the
fulfillment Cloud Function

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.000
within Dialogflow
and click Deploy.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.760
Now we are ready to test.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.510
In the simulator on the right,
query your Dialogflow agent

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:30.010
with something like set an
appointment at 8:00 PM on 10th.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.610
And respond to the
follow up questions

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.940
to provide appointment
type information.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.120
I'm using license
as an example here.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.870
You will see an
appointment setup response

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.600
in the simulator, and it
will show up on the calendar

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.990
as well.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.970
Now navigate to BigQuery
and execute select star

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:52.840
from the table query to
verify that a rule was

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.140
added for your recent test
with the appointment type.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.200
From within BigQuery,
we can explore this data

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.690
to find interesting insights.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.820
Click on the Explore
in Data Studio

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.720
button, which takes
us to Data Studio.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.870
Here, say we want to know
how many appointments

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.910
are for license versus
registration versus others.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.700
It is as easy as
dragging the pie chart

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.910
and editing the
values we want to see.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:25.300
I'm also interested in knowing
how many total appointments are

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.410
set up and another bar
graph that tells me

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.390
appointment breakdown per day.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:35.860
Well, that is pretty amazing
in less than a minute.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.120
Let's summarize what
we learned today.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.870
We enhanced our appointment
scheduler Chatbot

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.850
by creating an
integration with BigQuery.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.200
We created a data set
and a table in BigQuery

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.660
and connected it
to our fulfillment.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.930
Then we tested our agent
to confirm that the data is

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:54.910
being sent to BigQuery.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.670
Finally, we were able
to explore the data

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:00.670
in Data Studio through
one click explore feature

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.020
within BigQuery.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:03.990
Comment below to
let us know how you

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.390
are trying this integration
in your specific use case.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.280
I will see you in
the next episode

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.930
of Deconstructing Chatbots.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.480
Until then, please like and
subscribe to our channel

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.840
for more such content.

